<!-- ============================== §01 ============================== -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Problem</div>
<h2>Loss Spikes That Disappear with a Single Precision Cast</h2>
<p>Train a small transformer or MLP with cross-entropy loss and no weight decay — long enough to watch the training loss reach zero. You'll see something unexpected: the loss abruptly spikes back toward random-guessing level, then recovers, then spikes again. Periodically. This is the <strong>Slingshot Mechanism</strong>, first documented in grokking experiments and widely assumed to be a property of adaptive optimizers like Adam interacting with the loss landscape.</p>

<p>The paper's key result is stark: <span class="precision-badge f32">float32</span> shows periodic spikes. Switch to <span class="precision-badge f64">float64</span> — or just cast the logits to float64 <em>only inside the loss computation</em> while keeping all model parameters in float32 — and the spikes disappear entirely. The model parameters themselves never change precision.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">training-loss-curves.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">toggle precision to compare</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="vp-controls">
      <button class="vp-btn active" id="curve-btn-f32">float32 (spikes)</button>
      <button class="vp-btn" id="curve-btn-f64">float64 (stable)</button>
      <button class="vp-btn" id="curve-btn-both">Compare both</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="loss-canvas" style="width:100%;height:240px;display:block;border-radius:6px;background:var(--card2)"></canvas>
    <div style="font-size:.68rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.5rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono)">Modular division task (prime p=97), 2-layer Transformer, Adam, zero weight decay. Loss on log scale.</div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The spikes are not a quirk of a specific optimizer or architecture. They appear across MLPs, VGG11, and ViT on CIFAR-10, and across Transformer models on modular arithmetic. They vanish identically when the only change is floating-point precision in the log-softmax computation. This paper proves why.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>Key finding:</strong> Casting only the output logits to float64 during loss computation — while all model weights remain in float32 — is sufficient to eliminate all Slingshot loss spikes. The cause is in the loss computation, not in the optimizer or the weight space.
</div>
</section>

<!-- ============================== §02 ============================== -->
<section id="s-sc" data-code="sc">
<div class="section-no">02 — Softmax Collapse</div>
<h2>When the Correct-Class Gradient Rounds to Exactly Zero</h2>
<p>The cross-entropy loss uses the Log-Sum-Exp trick for numerical stability. PyTorch computes the denominator as:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Log-Sum-Exp denominator</div>
  <span class="math-var">Z</span> = log(Σ exp(<span class="math-var">z</span><sub>k</sub>)) = <span class="math-var">z</span><sub>m</sub> + log(Σ exp(<span class="math-var">z</span><sub>k</sub> − <span class="math-var">z</span><sub>m</sub>))<br>
  <span class="math-comment">where z_m = max_k z_k (the maximum logit, usually the correct class late in training)</span>
</div>

<p>In IEEE 754 float32, mantissa precision is 24 bits. When you add two numbers a and b where |b|/|a| &lt; 2⁻²³ ≈ 1.19×10⁻⁷, the smaller value simply disappears — it gets absorbed. This is called an <strong>absorption error</strong>. Applied to the log-sum-exp: when the correct-class logit z<sub>m</sub> exceeds all other logits by more than <strong>23 × ln(2) ≈ 15.94</strong>, every exp(z<sub>k</sub> − z<sub>m</sub>) for k ≠ m underflows to zero, so Z = z<sub>m</sub>.</p>

<p>Now look at the gradient of the correct-class logit z<sub>r</sub>:</p>
<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Gradient under Softmax Collapse</div>
  <span class="math-var">g</span><sub>r</sub> = ŷ<sub>r</sub> − y<sub>r</sub> = e<sup>z<sub>r</sub>−Z</sup> − 1 = e<sup>z<sub>r</sub>−z<sub>r</sub></sup> − 1 = e⁰ − 1 = <strong>0</strong><br>
  <span class="math-comment">But for incorrect classes k≠r: g_k = exp(z_k − z_r) ≠ 0 (small, but not zero)</span>
</div>

<p>This is <strong>Softmax Collapse (SC)</strong> — the training loss computes to zero, the correct class gradient is identically zero, but the incorrect classes still have nonzero gradients. Prior work (Prieto et al. 2025) identified SC and hypothesized it merely halts generalization. This paper shows SC has a second, more dangerous effect.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">softmax-collapse.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">adjust confidence to see gradient vanish</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="vp-controls">
      <button class="vp-btn active" id="sc-btn-low">Low confidence (gap=5)</button>
      <button class="vp-btn" id="sc-btn-high">High confidence (gap=17)</button>
      <button class="vp-btn" id="sc-btn-ultra">Ultra (gap=25)</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="sc-canvas" style="width:100%;height:260px;display:block;border-radius:6px;background:var(--card2)"></canvas>
    <div style="font-size:.68rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.5rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono)">float32 threshold: gap &gt; 23 × ln(2) ≈ 15.94 → correct-class gradient rounds to exactly 0</div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>For float64, the threshold is 52 × ln(2) ≈ 36.04 — achievable only with absurdly large logits that standard training never reaches. This is why switching to float64 is a complete cure.</p>
</section>

<!-- ============================== §03 ============================== -->
<section id="s-zerosum" data-code="zerosum">
<div class="section-no">03 — Zero-Sum Breaks</div>
<h2>The Constraint That Softmax Collapse Violates</h2>
<p>In exact arithmetic, the gradients across all K classes satisfy a strict zero-sum constraint. For any input x with feature h:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Zero-sum constraint (exact arithmetic)</div>
  Σ<sub>k=1..K</sub> ∇<sub>W_k</sub> L = Σ<sub>k</sub> (ŷ<sub>k</sub> − y<sub>k</sub>) · <span class="math-var">h</span> = (Σ<sub>k</sub> ŷ<sub>k</sub> − Σ<sub>k</sub> y<sub>k</sub>) · <span class="math-var">h</span> = <strong>0</strong><br>
  <span class="math-comment">Because Σ softmax probs = 1 = Σ one-hot labels</span>
</div>

<p>This means the <strong>global classifier mean</strong> W<sub>G</sub> = (1/K) Σ<sub>k</sub> W<sub>k</sub> never changes under gradient descent — it's an exact invariant of training under CE loss in exact arithmetic.</p>

<p>Softmax Collapse kills this invariant. Under SC, the correct-class gradient is zero, so the sum becomes only the incorrect-class contributions: Σ<sub>k≠r</sub> ŷ<sub>k</sub> · h = ε · h, where ε = Σ<sub>k≠r</sub> ŷ<sub>k</sub> is the residual probability on incorrect classes (small but nonzero). The gradient update now imparts a drift on W<sub>G</sub>:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Theorem 3.4 — Drift of global classifier mean</div>
  E[ΔW<sub>G</sub>] = − (η · ε / K) · <span class="math-var">μ</span><sub>G</sub><br>
  <span class="math-comment">η = learning rate, ε = residual probability, μ_G = global feature mean</span>
</div>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">zero-sum-constraint.svg</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">compare normal vs SC gradient contributions</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="vp-controls">
      <button class="vp-btn active" id="zs-btn-normal">Normal (exact arithmetic)</button>
      <button class="vp-btn" id="zs-btn-sc">After Softmax Collapse</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="zs-canvas" style="width:100%;height:240px;display:block;border-radius:6px;background:var(--card2)"></canvas>
    <div style="font-size:.68rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.5rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono)">K=3 classes (cat / dog / bird). Arrows show gradient update direction on each weight row W_k. W_G = mean of all W_k.</div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The drift direction is −μ<sub>G</sub>, pointing opposite the global feature mean. This is the seed of the problem. But W<sub>G</sub> becoming nonzero doesn't immediately cause a spike — it triggers something worse: a feedback loop.</p>
</section>

<!-- ============================== §04 ============================== -->
<section id="s-nfi" data-code="nfi">
<div class="section-no">04 — Numerical Feature Inflation</div>
<h2>The Feedback Loop That Grows Exponentially</h2>
<p>Once W<sub>G</sub> starts drifting toward −μ<sub>G</sub>, the geometry of the classifier changes. The weight vectors W<sub>k</sub> now have a global mean component W<sub>G</sub> that points in the −μ<sub>G</sub> direction. This creates a secondary effect on the feature gradients (Proposition 3.6):</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Proposition 3.6 — Feature gradient has a W_G component</div>
  Proj<sub>W_G</sub>(∇<sub>h</sub> L) = ε · W<sub>G</sub><br>
  <span class="math-comment">The feature update drifts in the direction of W_G → features pushed toward −W_G → toward +μ_G</span>
</div>

<p>Now we have a coupled feedback system:</p>
<ul>
  <li>W<sub>G</sub> drifts toward −μ<sub>G</sub></li>
  <li>μ<sub>G</sub> drifts toward −W<sub>G</sub></li>
  <li>They become anti-parallel (cos → −1)</li>
  <li>Each reinforces the other → <strong>exponential growth</strong></li>
</ul>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Theorem 3.7 — NFI exponential growth</div>
  ‖W<sub>G</sub><sup>(t)</sup>‖ ∝ (1 + η·ε / √K)^t<br>
  ‖μ<sub>G</sub><sup>(t)</sup>‖ ∝ (1 + η·ε / √K)^t<br>
  cos(W<sub>G</sub><sup>(t)</sup>, μ<sub>G</sub><sup>(t)</sup>) → −1 &nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.75rem">(anti-parallel)</span>
</div>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">nfi-feedback-loop.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">advance epochs to see exponential growth</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="vp-controls">
      <button class="vp-btn active" id="nfi-btn-0">epoch 0</button>
      <button class="vp-btn" id="nfi-btn-300">epoch 300</button>
      <button class="vp-btn" id="nfi-btn-700">epoch 700</button>
      <button class="vp-btn" id="nfi-btn-1200">epoch 1200 ⚡</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="nfi-canvas" style="width:100%;height:280px;display:block;border-radius:6px;background:var(--card2)"></canvas>
    <div style="font-size:.68rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.5rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono)">W_G (purple) and μ_G (cyan) vectors in 2D. As NFI develops, they become anti-parallel and grow exponentially. At epoch 1200, some training samples lose their SC margin → spike imminent.</div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Critically, NFI requires ε to stay small but nonzero. If ε decays too fast (like ResNet18, where the model quickly pushes all samples deep into SC), the feedback factor (1 + ηε/√K)<sup>t</sup> stays near 1 and NFI can't develop. This is why ResNet18 is immune: it learns fast enough to escape before the feedback loop takes hold.</p>

<div class="callout cyan">
  <strong>The NFI condition:</strong> NFI can only develop when ε is small but approximately constant — the model is very confident (so SC applies) but not learning quickly (so ε doesn't decay). This is precisely the regime after the model memorizes training data but before it generalizes.
</div>
</section>

<!-- ============================== §05 ============================== -->
<section id="s-spike" data-code="spike">
<div class="section-no">05 — The Spike Trigger</div>
<h2>How Adam Turns a Precision Error into a Loss Explosion</h2>
<p>NFI doesn't immediately cause a loss spike — it creates the conditions for one. Here is the precise sequence that triggers a Slingshot spike:</p>

<div class="step-list" id="spike-steps">
  <div class="step-box active">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Model enters Softmax Collapse regime</div>
      <div class="step-desc">After long training, all samples have correct-class logit margin &gt; 15.94 (float32 threshold). Correct-class gradient rounds to zero. Loss computes to zero. But ε = Σ<sub>k≠r</sub> ŷ<sub>k</sub> ≈ 3×10⁻⁹ remains nonzero.</div>
      <div class="step-code">margin = z_correct - max(z_other) &gt; 23 * ln(2) ≈ 15.94</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">NFI feedback loop starts</div>
      <div class="step-desc">W<sub>G</sub> begins drifting toward −μ<sub>G</sub>. The feature mean μ<sub>G</sub> responds by drifting toward −W<sub>G</sub>. Both norms grow. The cosine similarity between them approaches −1.</div>
      <div class="step-code">‖W_G‖ grows as (1 + η·ε/√K)^t — each epoch multiplies by ~1.003</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Adam's effective learning rate inflates</div>
      <div class="step-desc">With correct-class gradient ≈ 0, Adam's second moment v<sub>t</sub> ≈ 9×10⁻¹⁸ decays toward zero. The effective step size η/√(v<sub>t</sub>+ε<sub>Adam</sub>) grows toward its maximum η/ε<sub>Adam</sub> = 0.001/10⁻⁸ = 10⁵.</div>
      <div class="step-code">effective_lr = η / sqrt(v_t + ε_Adam) → η / ε_Adam = 10^5</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">4</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Sample margins collapse — gradient re-emerges</div>
      <div class="step-desc">As W<sub>G</sub> and μ<sub>G</sub> grow anti-parallel, some outlier samples near the decision boundary lose their SC margin. Their correct-class gradient suddenly jumps from 0 to ~1.19×10⁻⁷ (just above the float32 floor). Adam's moment estimates lag this sudden change.</div>
      <div class="step-code">margin of outlier &lt; 15.94 → gradient: 0 → 1.19e-7</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">5</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Adam fires at 40× magnitude → loss spike</div>
      <div class="step-desc">First moment: m = 0.9 × 3×10⁻⁹ + 0.1 × 1.19×10⁻⁷ = 1.46×10⁻⁸. Second moment: v = 0.95 × 9×10⁻¹⁸ + 0.05 × (1.19×10⁻⁷)² = 2.7×10⁻⁸. Update = η·m/√v = <strong>4×10⁻⁴</strong>, vs normal 1×10⁻⁵. The model parameters are displaced by 40×, predictions become random, loss spikes to ~2.3.</div>
      <div class="step-code">update = η × m / sqrt(v) = 4e-4 ≈ 40× normal magnitude</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="step-nav">
  <button id="step-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
  <span id="step-prog" style="font-size:.7rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">Step 1 of 5</span>
  <button id="step-next">Next →</button>
</div>

<h3>Why Vanilla GD Also Spikes</h3>
<p>The paper verifies that the spike is not Adam-specific. After training loss hits zero, switching to vanilla Gradient Descent with a manually-inflated learning rate η = 10⁵ (to emulate Adam's large effective step size) triggers a Slingshot spike at approximately the same epoch. The difference: Adam recovers from the spike (forming the periodic pattern), while GD diverges permanently.</p>
</section>

