<div class="callout amber">
  <strong>Layer Normalization warning:</strong> Applying LN directly before the classifier <em>accelerates</em> Slingshot onset. LN constrains feature norms, so even small angular misalignment crosses the SC threshold faster. It also induces stepwise norm growth rather than gradual growth. Avoid using LN as a stability fix without BatchNorm.
</div>

<h3>NFI in Large Language Models</h3>
<p>Softmax Collapse also appears during GPT pre-training on FineWeb. In each training step with ~130,000 tokens, roughly 4,000 tokens have exactly zero loss under float32. These are highly predictable tokens — not just the most frequent, but those appearing in templates: <code>"example"</code>, <code>"org"</code>, <code>"last"</code> (as in "last name"), <code>"."</code>.</p>

<p>Long LLM training shows abnormal logit growth — mean token logits growing from ~5 to ~183 after 100k steps under float32. Interestingly, switching to float64 <em>increases</em> this to 498. Unlike classification tasks with balanced classes, language modeling has Zipf-distributed token frequencies. The resulting imbalance in output embeddings creates a large W<sub>G</sub> even without NFI — and NFI's anti-parallel suppression actually slows logit divergence. This makes the LLM setting more nuanced than the classification case, but the core finding holds: <strong>removing the last-layer mean W<sub>G</sub> stabilizes logit growth</strong> in both settings.</p>

<div class="callout green">
  <strong>Practical recommendation:</strong> For unregularized training that reaches high confidence (classification without weight decay, grokking experiments), the most robust fixes are: (1) apply BatchNorm immediately before the final linear layer, or (2) enforce the zero-sum gradient constraint by subtracting (1/K)Σg<sub>k</sub> from each class logit gradient. For LLM pre-training, centering the output embedding weight matrix (removing W<sub>G</sub>) reduces late-stage logit growth.
</div>
</section>

